+# Spends money
+
+## Add at intake.questions
+
+For a part whose actions cost real money (a paid API, a purchase, a metered model or service), ask before design, and write each answer to the intake notes: which actions cost money, on which **platform and account** each is charged; the **worst case** of each, the most one run of it can cost when it goes wrong as well as when it goes right (every retry, every item of the largest input, a run that does not stop), estimated in that platform's own units and in money; and the **cap** the owner sets, the most the project may spend in all, per month or per run as the owner names it, with what happens at the cap (the action stops and the owner is asked; never a silent overrun). The loop's own paid actions (a contract capture, a dry or paid run of an increment, a re-recording, a benchmark against a paid service) are tracked against the cap by the supervisor; where the product itself spends in the owner's use, the design states how the product enforces the cap, and the cap is a value the owner chose, fixed in a test (rule 52). This trait adds no design section, so the intake notes carry its two duties to the design as requirements of it: how the product enforces the cap, where it spends in the owner's use, and the folder that holds the store of paid results (its addition at `configuration.items`). No paid action of the loop runs without the owner's approval first, given in words for that action, with its estimate against the worst case and the platform and account named in the same sentence; an approval covers the action it names, never the next one. A paid call is a remote call, whose wait the trait *external services* bounds (its addition at `delegation-loop.inspect-checks`), so a part that pays a remote service is classified with *external services* too, which the design gate's trait decision checks.
+
+## Add at configuration.items
+
+**The store of paid results.** Before the first paid action, configure where its results are kept: a folder the design names, which no clean, reset, cache purge or test teardown reaches, each result kept with the request that bought it, so that a retry, a rerun or a crash reads the result already paid for instead of buying it again. Its path is a Resources row of `docs/INVENTORY.md`. **The cap** is recorded where the owner can read it: a Decisions row `Spending cap: <amount> <per month or per run> on <platform and account> (<the owner's words>, <date>)`, beside the provider's own spending limit in the account's Resources row, which configuration's spending-limits item sets.
+
+## Add at delegation-loop.brief-constraints
+
+The builder runs no paid action: a brief that needs one says so, and the paid run is the supervisor's, after the owner's approval for it. Every paid call the part's code makes goes through the store of paid results: its result written there with the request that bought it, and the store read before a call is bought, so a retry never buys twice. Before any paid run, the brief's increment has run once **dry**: on a copy of the data, with a stand-in provider that answers as the real one does (its recorded replies, the trait *external services*' contract capture) and charges nothing, the run's every paid call counted and compared with the estimate. The first paid run of a contract capture, which has no recorded replies to stand in yet, and a paid action with no remote service behind it (a purchase) run instead at the smallest size that answers them, under their own approval, their paid calls counted beforehand against the estimate.
+
+## Add at delegation-loop.inspect-checks
+
+No paid action ran unapproved, and none bypassed the store: the supervisor reads the run's log and the store of paid results, and the provider's usage for the run's time where a Resources row records a way to read it (otherwise the owner reads it at the stage close, by a run sheet); a paid call with no approval naming it, or a paid call in the diff that does not both read the store first and write its result there, is a blocking finding, a paid call that ran unapproved also recorded as an issue and told to the owner in the next report's first line with its amount, platform and account.
